{
  "summary": "This study investigates kinetic-environmental coupling at buried interfaces in ambient flexible perovskite solar cells. Using dual-side in situ photoluminescence, it reveals simultaneous nucleation at gas-liquid and liquid-solid interfaces during processing. The research identifies outward solvent evaporation delaying α-phase conversion at the buried interface, causing kinetic mismatch that worsens moisture damage and defects. A self-segregating molecule (NFA) was designed to synchronize crystallization, passivate defects, and create a hydrophobic barrier, achieving 24.84% power conversion efficiency in small cells and 21.46% in mini-modules with stable performance after 1000h damp aging.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Simultaneous nucleation occurs at both gas-liquid and liquid-solid interfaces during antisolvent processing",
    "Outward solvent evaporation delays α-phase conversion at the buried interface, exacerbating moisture damage",
    "Self-segregating molecule NFA synchronizes crystallization, passivates defects, and forms a hydrophobic barrier"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes self-segregated interfacial regulation as a rational strategy to overcome kinetic-environmental constraints in scalable flexible perovskite photovoltaics."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates why early humans settled only briefly in 'Green Arabia' despite abundant freshwater. Using a 1,500-km geoarchaeological survey across the Rub' al Khali desert, researchers found no lithic artifacts or knappable stone at 55 of 125 sites with paleolake deposits. The results indicate that early human settlement was constrained by the availability of stone for tool production, not water alone, suggesting a dual requirement of water and lithic resources for habitation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "No lithic artifacts or knappable stone were found at 55 of 125 sites with paleolake deposits",
    "Spatial modeling shows 57% of water-accessible terrain lacks knappable stone",
    "Convergent patterns across Africa, North America, and Asia indicate stone availability, not water, was the primary constraint on early human dispersal"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates that early human settlement in arid regions required both water and lithic raw materials, challenging models that prioritize water availability alone and highlighting the role of geological constraints in human dispersal."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how protein kinase A (PKA) signaling regulates group 3 innate lymphoid cell (ILC3) homeostasis and inflammation in the gut. Using a single-cell atlas, researchers found that prostaglandin activates PKA to promote tissue-protective functions, while interleukin-23 (IL-23) suppresses PKA inhibitors. Deletion of PRKAR1A reduced ILC3s and increased infection susceptibility in mice. IL-23 overproduction decreased PRKAR1A, increasing PKA signaling and gut inflammation. Human ILC3s express PRKAR1A, which is downregulated by IL-23 or in inflammatory bowel disease.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Prostaglandin activates PKA to upregulate tissue-protective factors in ILC3s",
    "IL-23 decreases PRKAR1A, a PKA inhibitor, in ILC3s",
    "IL-23 overproduction reduces ILC3s and triggers gut inflammation via PKA hyperactivity"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes PKA signaling as a critical regulator of ILC3 homeostasis, revealing how IL-23-driven PKA dysregulation contributes to intestinal inflammation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates iron's melting temperature and resistivity under Earth core pressures using microsecond pulsed Joule heating in diamond anvil cells. Over 300 reproducible melting measurements reveal a shallow melting curve, reaching 6 K/GPa and 4160 ± 80 K at 200 GPa. The findings suggest a cold core-mantle boundary (3430 K) and low adiabatic outer core heat flow (9.8 TW), implying thermal convection could power the geodynamo before inner core formation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A shallow melting curve for iron at 200 GPa, reaching 4160 ± 80 K",
    "Inferred cold core-mantle boundary at 3430 K",
    "Low adiabatic outer core heat flow of 9.8 TW"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides critical constraints on Earth's core thermal structure, suggesting thermal convection alone could drive the geodynamo prior to inner core formation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates shear alignment polymer extrusion (SHAPE) 3D printing for fabricating high-loading nanocomposites with ordered nanostructures. The approach uses high shear forces to align nanofillers like boron nitride and carbon nanotubes in polymer matrices, enabling scalable production of complex geometries. Results show 55 wt% boron nitride composites with zero volume shrinkage, exceptional thermal stability, and tailored thermal conductivity (1.07 W/m/K) and mechanical properties.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SHAPE 3D printing achieves 55 wt% boron nitride loading in polylactic acid composites",
    "Aligned nanocomposites exhibit zero volume shrinkage and thermal stability above polymer melting temperatures",
    "Vertically aligned structures show enhanced thermal conductivity (1.07 W/m/K) while horizontally aligned ones provide thermal insulation (0.071 W/m/K)"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a scalable method for creating nanocomposites with high nanofiller loadings and long-range ordered nanostructures, advancing cross-scale design for thermal management and structural applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether brief dietary changes during acute inflammation influence long-term tissue immune memory. Using lean mice with type 17 skin inflammation, researchers found that a transient high-fat diet (HFD) exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates inflammation to levels seen in chronic obesity. HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window prevents amplification. Mechanistically, HFD recruits neutrophils, which express Nlrp3 and Il1b, activating a neutrophil-NLRP3-IL-1β axis that licenses pathogenic TH17 cells. After recovery, chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cells persist, causing exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence despite subsequent low-fat diet. The findings demonstrate that short-lived dietary states during specific temporal windows can durably reprogram immune responses.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Transient high-fat diet exposure within a 4-day window at disease onset exacerbates acute type 17 skin inflammation to chronic obesity levels",
    "HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window prevents inflammatory amplification",
    "Neutrophil recruitment and NLRP3-IL-1β axis activation license pathogenic TH17 cells, persisting as chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cells"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes that transient dietary states during narrow temporal windows can durably reprogram immune memory, revealing a mechanism by which short-lived physiological changes influence long-term inflammatory responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated early Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak dynamics in the Democratic Republic of the Congo during May 2026. Using case records and human mobility data, researchers analyzed spatial expansion from May 10th to June 1st. They found human movement drove the spread, increasing reported cases across 12 health zones and 49 health areas within ~10 days of doubling time. The rapid expansion highlights critical timing for intervention strategies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Human mobility was a primary driver of early spatial expansion in the Bundibugyo virus outbreak",
    "The outbreak expanded to 12 health zones and 49 health areas within 10 days of doubling time",
    "Early outbreak dynamics showed rapid spatial growth with implications for timely intervention strategies"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates how human movement patterns critically influence early viral spread dynamics, providing insights for optimizing outbreak response timing in resource-limited settings."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the long-standing challenge of achieving stable photocatalytic pure water splitting over Ta3N5 by identifying incompatibility in cocatalysts between alkaline water oxidation and pure water splitting systems. The researchers developed a highly dispersed RuCoOx cocatalyst using a multi-step incipient wetness impregnation strategy. The optimized Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx photocatalyst achieved stoichiometric water splitting with an apparent quantum yield of 0.72% at 420 nm, the highest reported for Ta-based nitrides. Mechanistic insights indicate enhanced interfacial electric fields and reduced reaction barriers improve hole extraction and intermediate kinetics.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Identification of cocatalyst incompatibility as the critical bottleneck for stable pure water splitting over Ta3N5",
    "Development of a highly dispersed RuCoOx cocatalyst via multi-step incipient wetness impregnation",
    "Optimized Pt@Cr2O3/Ta3N5/RuCoOx achieved 0.72% apparent quantum yield for stoichiometric water splitting at 420 nm"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes cocatalyst compatibility as a fundamental design principle for efficient photocatalytic pure water splitting in nitride and oxynitride materials, providing mechanistic insights for advancing solar-driven water splitting technologies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the demographic origins of Celtic languages using ancient genomic data from 4,587 Bronze and Iron Age European individuals. By analyzing genome-wide data, the researchers identified three key population movements: Steppe-related ancestry arriving via Bell Beaker groups around 4400 BP, a Bronze Age migration from southwestern Europe to Britain, and a westward expansion of eastern central European ancestry (3200-2800 BP) affecting France, the British Isles, and Italy. The findings suggest that the eastern central European migration is the most parsimonious explanation for Celtic language spread, though language shifts may not directly correlate with genetic changes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Steppe-related ancestry entered Europe via Bell Beaker groups approximately 4400 years ago",
    "Bronze Age migration from southwestern Europe to Britain occurred between 3800-3200 BP",
    "Eastern central European ancestry expanded westward from 3200-2800 BP, influencing regions where Celtic languages later emerged"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This research provides genomic evidence supporting a specific demographic pathway for Celtic language dispersal in prehistoric Europe, clarifying the role of population movements in language spread despite potential decoupling between genetic and linguistic evolution."
}

{
  "summary": "This study assesses whether Earth's cryosphere remains within a safe operating space (SOS) under Anthropocene change using the planetary boundaries framework. It finds accelerating, partly irreversible cryospheric loss driven by feedbacks, with key variables departing Holocene conditions. The cryosphere has breached its SOS, posing long-term risks to human societies and ecosystems, necessitating integrated Earth system management.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Accelerating and partly irreversible loss of cryospheric integrity",
    "Key cryospheric control variables have departed or are committed to departing Holocene-like conditions",
    "The cryosphere has breached its safe operating space, increasing long-term risks for human societies and ecosystems"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This research establishes that cryosphere change has exceeded planetary safe operating limits, highlighting critical thresholds in Earth system resilience that require integrated management to prevent irreversible ecological and societal consequences."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a hydrogel strategy for Candida vaginitis treatment by integrating pathogen clearance with vaginal microecosystem restoration. The engineered HCS/FUC@LabPC hydrogel degrades in the vaginal microenvironment, releasing chitosan oligosaccharides to eliminate Candida albicans via cell wall disruption and MAPK signaling interference. Fucoidan and procyanidine reduce inflammation and promote Lactobacillus colonization, restoring microbial balance and barrier function in murine models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The hydrogel degrades in vaginal microenvironments to release chitosan oligosaccharides that disrupt Candida albicans cell walls",
    "Fucoidan and procyanidine reduce inflammation and support Lactobacillus colonization",
    "The approach breaks the infection-dysbiosis-recurrence cycle by restoring microbial balance and barrier integrity"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This strategy provides a dual-action mechanism for eradicating pathogens while simultaneously restoring vaginal microbiome homeostasis, addressing a critical gap in treating recurrent candidiasis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated viral-bacterial interactions during algal bloom collapse using a synthetic community model. Researchers quantified how bacteria enhance virus-induced mortality in coccolithophores and microalgae, identifying Alteromonas as a key player. The synergy index and benefit index revealed bacterial-viral co-infection drives bloom termination, impacting carbon cycling pathways by balancing viral shunt and shuttle processes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Bacteria significantly increase virus-induced algal mortality in coccolithophores and microalgae",
    "Alteromonas bacteria exhibit high chemoattraction to infected cells and accumulate during bloom collapse",
    "Viral-bacterial synergy modulates carbon cycling by balancing viral shunt and shuttle pathways"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies bacterial-viral synergy as a previously unrecognized mechanism controlling algal bloom termination and carbon cycling dynamics in marine ecosystems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study constructed a comprehensive atlas of the tumor microenvironment in estrogen-receptor positive ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) using single-cell RNA sequencing and spatial transcriptomics. Analyzing 854,911 cells from 159 patients and 39 samples, it identified 9 cell types and 60 cell states. The findings reveal remodeling of fibroblasts, vascular, and basal cells surrounding DCIS regions, along with immunosuppressive ducts that support preinvasive cancer growth, indicating early alterations persisting through invasive progression.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Identification of 9 cell types and 60 cell states in DCIS tumor microenvironment via single-cell RNA sequencing",
    "Remodeling of fibroblasts, vascular, and basal cells encircling DCIS regions",
    "Presence of immunosuppressive ducts supporting preinvasive cancer growth, with alterations persisting during invasive progression"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides the first detailed cellular atlas of estrogen-receptor positive DCIS, revealing early microenvironmental changes that may inform understanding of preinvasive cancer progression and therapeutic targeting."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates mechanically-driven reconstruction of lithium metal in sulfide-based all-solid-state batteries to address dendrite growth. By folding and rolling commercial lithium, the research achieves surface refinement and texture homogenization, enhancing interfacial stability. At high folding degrees, it suppresses dendrites and void formation, enabling stable cycling with 79.3% capacity retention after 1000 cycles.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Mechanical folding and rolling of lithium metal refines surface texture and replaces carbonate-rich layers with oxide-passivated surfaces",
    "High-degree reconstruction homogenizes texture, reduces interfacial spatial variations, and synchronizes lithium recession and advancement",
    "Stable cycling with 79.3% capacity retention after 1000 cycles at C/2 rate and 0.05% per-cycle decay"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "Mechanical reconstruction provides a direct materials-level strategy to stabilize lithium metal in sulfide-based all-solid-state batteries by suppressing dendrite growth and enhancing interfacial stability."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether aggression experience and observation engage common neural mechanisms to shape behaviour. Using longitudinal cell-type-specific recordings in subcortical networks, researchers compared animals trained via direct aggression or observation. Results show both approaches induce shared adaptive behavioural strategies and neural changes, including reduced inhibition in a core aggression network, persisting even in novel contexts.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Aggression observation recruits widespread subcortical networks similar to experienced behaviour",
    "Shared neural changes, including reduced inhibition in a core aggression network, persist across training types",
    "Both experience and observation induce a common adaptive behavioural strategy absent in untrained animals"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The findings reveal a shared inhibitory plasticity mechanism that enables adaptive behavioural strategies through either aggression experience or observation, providing insight into the neural basis of social learning."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated the long-term circulation of an Andes virus (ANDV) lineage in northern Patagonia, linking it to a 2026 cruise ship hantavirus pulmonary syndrome (HPS) outbreak. Retrospective analysis showed a common ANDV lineage spanning HPS cases from 2018 to 2025 in a geographically defined region, with person-to-person transmission in the 2018 outbreak. The cruise ship outbreak's infection source remains unresolved due to inconsistent exposure timing with ANDV incubation periods, underscoring the need for enhanced viral surveillance in ANDV-endemic areas.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A single ANDV lineage persisted in northern Patagonia from 2018 to 2025, causing HPS cases.",
    "The 2018 outbreak involved person-to-person transmission of ANDV.",
    "The 2026 cruise ship outbreak's source could not be identified due to timing inconsistencies with ANDV incubation periods."
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates sustained ANDV circulation in a specific geographic region over seven years, revealing complex transmission dynamics that challenge assumptions about hantavirus epidemiology in endemic areas."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed an in situ lab-on-fiber platform to monitor cadmium sulfide photocorrosion dynamics via dual-mode optoelectronic tracking. The platform correlated transmission spectra with photocurrent changes, revealing nonlinear light intensity dependence, minimal photocorrosion rates at 0.8 mW·cm⁻² UV, and suppression via 0.4 V bias. It also identified a protective sulfur cycle from sacrificial agents, enabling catalyst optimization and broader applications in nanomaterial interfacial studies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CdS photocorrosion shows nonlinear dependence on light intensity",
    "Photocorrosion rate is minimized at 0.8 mW·cm⁻² UV light",
    "Applying 0.4 V bias suppresses photocorrosion and enables a protective sulfur cycle"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The platform provides real-time, dual-mode monitoring of photocorrosion dynamics in nanomaterials, advancing the design of stable catalysts and interfacial mechanisms for energy applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study used a dual-sgRNA CRISPR interference platform to map genetic interactions among essential genes in Mycobacter: M. smegmatis and M. tuberculosis. The approach overcame technical challenges in library construction and data analysis to generate comprehensive interaction maps. Results revealed known biological relationships, thousands of novel interactions, and functional links between essential genes and drug targets, with some interactions predicted to reflect physical protein associations.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Comprehensive genetic interaction maps of essential genes in Mycobacterium tuberculosis and M. smegmatis",
    "Identification of thousands of previously unrecognized gene interactions",
    "Functional connections between essential genes and drug targets, with AlphaFold3 predictions supporting physical protein associations"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a generalizable framework for essential-gene network analysis in mycobacteria, advancing understanding of tuberculosis pathogenesis and informing combination therapy design through systematic genetic interaction mapping."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated a hantavirus pulmonary syndrome (HPS) outbreak linked to cruise ship travel in April-May 2026, caused by Andes virus (ANDV). Genomic analysis of 10 cases revealed a tightly clustered outbreak cluster, indicating a single or few zoonotic spillover events followed by human-to-human transmission onboard. The outbreak demonstrates ANDV's capacity for sustained human transmission under specific conditions without novel strain emergence.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Genomic sequencing of 10 cases confirmed a single or few zoonotic spillover events before the outbreak",
    "Human-to-human transmission occurred onboard the cruise ship",
    "The outbreak did not involve a novel viral strain but demonstrated ANDV's established capability for sustained human transmission"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This outbreak confirms Andes virus's ability to transmit among humans through multiple spillover events and sustained person-to-person transmission under specific conditions, without requiring viral adaptation."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses zinc plating instability in zinc batteries by engineering copper current collectors with a sacrificial cuprous chloride interface. Chemical etching creates micro-textured copper that enhances electrochemical surface area and facilitates chloride-mediated interfacial regulation. The approach enables stable zinc plating at unity negative-to-positive capacity ratio, achieving 1654-hour cycling and high-rate performance with 70% capacity retention after 500 cycles.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Chemical etching of copper forms a micro-textured substrate with increased electrochemical surface area",
    "Chloride ions regulate zinc ion adsorption and suppress hydrogen evolution reactions",
    "Zn//Cu cells achieve 1654-hour cycling at unity N/P ratio with 70% capacity retention after 500 high-rate cycles"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a surface engineering strategy to overcome zinc plating limitations under material-limited conditions, enabling long-term cycling stability in zinc batteries."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies a conserved flowering-time module in barley, defined by a PHYTOCHROME C amino acid substitution and a winter-type VERNALIZATION 1 hapl:otype, that accelerates heading and maturity. The module, PhyC-VRN1, was selected during modern breeding in China to enable double-season cropping by escaping summer heat stress. The findings demonstrate functional conservation across Triticeae and validate precision genome editing for reconstructing climate-resilient crop adaptations.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A single amino acid substitution in PHYTOCHROME C and winter-type VERNALIZATION 1 haplotype define a flowering-time module in barley",
    "The PhyC-VRN1 module accelerates heading and maturity, enabling escape from summer heat stress in double-season cropping systems",
    "The module is functionally conserved across Triticeae and can be reconstructed via precision genome editing for climate-resilient crop design"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The identification and validation of a conserved flowering-time module that enables climate-resilient double-season cropping through precise genomic selection provides a foundational mechanism for breeding heat-tolerant, rotationally flexible crop varieties."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates electron viscosity in two-dimensional electron fluids using magnetoresistance in Corbino-shaped graphene devices. The approach disentangles ohmic and viscous transport contributions, revealing that the effective electron-electron scattering rate scales linearly with temperature in monolayer and bilayer graphene. This finding contradicts conventional Fermi liquid predictions and aligns with a tomographic fluid regime, suggesting non-Fermi liquid behavior in 2D electron systems.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Magnetoresistance in Corbusing graphene devices simultaneously measures ohmic and viscous transport contributions",
    "Effective electron-electron scattering rate scales linearly with temperature in monolayer and bilayer graphene",
    "The linear temperature dependence aligns with a tomographic fluid regime, not conventional Fermi liquid behavior"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate non-Fermi liquid behavior in two-dimensional electron systems, providing quantitative evidence for a tomographic fluid regime that challenges conventional transport models."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how 4f electron filling influences bonding evolution in lanthanide single-atom catalysts across the lanthanide series. Using structurally uniform catalysts, researchers identified a dual-periodic trend in binding energies, increasing from La to Eu and from Gd to Yb. The findings attribute this behavior to electron redistribution between 5d and 4f orbitals, with a sharp transition at the half-filled 4f7 configuration between Eu and Gd. The results demonstrate that this dual-periodic evolution is an intrinsic feature of lanthanide chemistry, providing a framework for understanding reactivity and catalyst design.",
  "key_findings": [
    "A dual-periodic trend in lanthanide-support binding energies increases from La to Eu and from Gd to Yb",
    "Electron redistribution between 5d and 4f orbitals modulates bonding energies, with a sharp transition at 4f7",
    "The dual-periodic evolution is an intrinsic feature of lanthanide chemistry, persisting across diverse coordination environments"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a 4f-electron-regulated bonding framework that explains lanthanide reactivity patterns and informs catalyst design strategies through intrinsic electronic characteristics."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates quantum quench dynamics in neutral-atom simulators with up to 180 qubits. By quenching across parameter spaces, researchers identified stable dynamical regimes and prethermal steady states stabilized over exponentially long timescales. They observed resonant melting phenomena and a prethermally driven first-order dynamical phase transition in two dimensions, linked to Néel defect proliferation. These findings demonstrate programmable access to high-energy prethermal phase structures.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Identification of stable dynamical regimes and prethermal steady states stabilized over exponentially long timescales",
    "Observation of resonant melting phenomena through sharp peaks in dynamical response",
    "Discovery of a prethermally driven first-order dynamical phase transition in two dimensions, associated with Néel defect proliferation"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a programmable method to explore high-energy prethermal phase structures, including long-lived dynamical phases and their resonant breakdown mechanisms."
}
